Recorded apt, apt-cache, dpkg-query and gpg output for Test-AptModule.

Recorded 2026-11-09 on an installed OS/7 1.0.0.277 (bench `ts`, amd64), as root,
under LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 — the locale powershell/Apt asks every program in, because
a machine installed in German otherwise answers "Holen:" (BUILD-NOTES #176).
recorded-with.txt names the versions: apt 3.2.1ubuntu0.1, gpg 2.4.8, dpkg 1.23.7.

<name>.out is stdout and stderr as one stream, as the recording shell captured
them; <name>.rc is the exit code. The module's parsers read both channels
joined, so a fixture with the two interleaved is the harder case, not a
simplification.

The vendor repository in them is Tailscale's (pkgs.tailscale.com, public).

  gpg-show-binary          gpg --show-keys --with-colons --with-fingerprint on
                           Tailscale's binary keyring: one primary, one subkey
  gpg-show-two             Microsoft's legacy keyring (one primary, no subkey)
  gpg-show-garbage         the same against a file that is not a key (rc 2)
  policy-pinned            apt-cache policy under the OS/7 vendor preference
                           (origin at -1, `tailscale` at 500): tailscale has a
                           candidate, tailscale-archive-keyring and
                           tailscale-nginx-auth have NONE, bash is installed,
                           and a package that does not exist prints nothing
  policy-installed-older   tailscale 1.104.4 installed, 1.104.5 the candidate
  policy-installed-newest  after `install --only-upgrade`
  install-only-upgrade     that install's output
  update-restricted-ok     `apt-get update --error-on=any` restricted to the
                           vendor source (Dir::Etc::SourceList=/dev/null,
                           Dir::Etc::SourceParts=<one file>)
  update-restricted-badkey the same with a keyring that does not hold the
                           signing key: NO_PUBKEY, rc 100
  update-restricted-badkey-noerroron
                           the same WITHOUT --error-on=any: the same E: lines,
                           and rc 0 — the reason the parser judges by the lines
                           as well as by the exit code
  dpkg-query               two installed packages and one that does not exist
                           (stderr first, rc 1)
  etc-apt/                 that machine's /etc/apt/sources.list, sources.list.d
                           and preferences.d, with the vendor source and
                           preference OS/7 writes

To re-record: the script is docs/SESSION-TAILSCALE.md's setting, the commands
are the ones above; keep the names, because Test-AptModule reads them by name.
